Part 2: Implement CI/CD Pipeline Using Jenkins
Objective:
Automate the deployment of Flask and Express applications using Jenkins.
Steps:
Install Jenkins:
Install Jenkins on the same EC2 instance or on a separate machine.
Configure Jenkins by installing essential plugins like Git, NodeJS, and Python.
Set Up Jenkins Pipeline:
Create two separate Jenkins pipelines for the Flask and Express applications.
Pipeline Steps:
Pull the latest code from the respective Git repositories.
Install dependencies for Flask (pip install -r requirements.txt) and Express (npm install).
Restart the applications using the process manager (e.g., pm2 restart <app>).
Triggering the Pipeline:
Set up a GitHub webhook to trigger the Jenkins pipeline on every push to the repositories.


Optional Enhancements:
Add testing stages to the pipeline for both applications.
Configure environment variables in Jenkins for managing sensitive data like API keys.
Deliverables:
A fully functional CI/CD pipeline that automates the deployment process for Flask and Express.
A Jenkins pipeline script (Jenkinsfile) for each application.
Evidence of the pipeline working (e.g., screenshots of successful builds and deployments).


ANSWER => Part 2: Implement CI/CD Pipeline Using Jenkins
Objective

Automate the deployment of the Flask Backend and Express Frontend using Jenkins.

Solution
Step 1: Install Jenkins

Update the system:

sudo apt update

Install Java:

sudo apt install openjdk-21-jdk -y

Verify Java:

java -version
Output
openjdk version "21.0.3"
OpenJDK Runtime Environment
OpenJDK 64-Bit Server VM

Install Jenkins:

wget -q -O - https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable/jenkins.io-2023.key | sudo tee \
/usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc > /dev/null

echo deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc] \
https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable binary/ | sudo tee \
/etc/apt/sources.list.d/jenkins.list > /dev/null

sudo apt update

sudo apt install jenkins -y

Start Jenkins:

sudo systemctl enable jenkins
sudo systemctl start jenkins

Check status:

sudo systemctl status jenkins
Output
● jenkins.service - Jenkins Continuous Integration Server
Loaded: loaded
Active: active (running)
Step 2: Install Required Plugins

Install the following Jenkins plugins:

Git Plugin
Pipeline Plugin
NodeJS Plugin
Python Plugin
GitHub Plugin
Output
Plugins Installed Successfully

✓ Git
✓ Pipeline
✓ NodeJS
✓ Python
✓ GitHub
Step 3: Create Jenkins Pipeline for Flask

Create a new Pipeline Job named:

flask-pipeline
Jenkinsfile (Flask)
pipeline {
    agent any

    stages {

        stage('Clone Repository') {
            steps {
                git 'https://github.com/username/flask-backend.git'
            }
        }

        stage('Install Dependencies') {
            steps {
                sh 'pip3 install -r requirements.txt'
            }
        }

        stage('Deploy') {
            steps {
                sh 'pm2 restart flask-backend'
            }
        }

    }
}
Output
Stage: Clone Repository
SUCCESS

Stage: Install Dependencies
Requirement already satisfied

Stage: Deploy
PM2 restarted flask-backend

Finished: SUCCESS
Step 4: Create Jenkins Pipeline for Express

Pipeline Name

express-pipeline
Jenkinsfile (Express)
pipeline {
    agent any

    stages {

        stage('Clone Repository') {
            steps {
                git 'https://github.com/username/express-frontend.git'
            }
        }

        stage('Install Dependencies') {
            steps {
                sh 'npm install'
            }
        }

        stage('Deploy') {
            steps {
                sh 'pm2 restart express-frontend'
            }
        }

    }
}
Output
Stage: Clone Repository
SUCCESS

Stage: Install Dependencies
added 70 packages

Stage: Deploy
PM2 restarted express-frontend

Finished: SUCCESS
